## 4. Missingness (likely direction of bias)
Outcome 1.2% missing; max-parental-ed 3.5%; ASVAB 12.2%; class rank 55.6%; 1978 income 19.6%; 1985 wealth 20.4%.
Selection is outcome-related: ASVAB missing 13.4% among non-attenders vs 7.3% among attenders; parental-ed
missing 4.0% vs 1.1%; class rank 61.3% vs 32.7%. Missingness also differs by race (ASVAB missing: NBH 14.2%,
BLACK 11.4%, HISP 7.8%). A logit on P(ASVAB missing) gives OR 0.59 (BLACK), 0.48 (HISP), 0.94 per parent-ed year,
1.18 per age year -> **MAR-violating, non-random**.
Sensitivity (mean-imputation + missing indicators) raises N 10,653 -> 12,532 and moves Black AOR 1.59 -> 1.53,
Hispanic 3.20 -> 2.69, parent-ed 1.184 -> 1.188; the indicator itself is protective (ASVAB-missing AOR 0.70).
**Likely direction of bias: complete-case analysis slightly inflates the positive Model-B race coefficients and
slightly understates the SES gradient, because attenders are over-represented among the observed-ASVAB rows; the
qualitative conclusions are unchanged.**

## 5. Robustness (y_O3, results/robustness_checks.csv)
Reversal and SES attenuation survive: age entered as dummies (Black 0.97 -> 1.62), each ASVAB score used singly
(Black 1.27-1.45), available-item ASVAB mean (1.59), + family size (1.55), restriction to age >= 18 in 1979
(Black 1.27 -> 2.45). VIFs of the four ASVAB scores 2.7-3.4, so Model B is not collinearity-degenerate.

## 6. Interpretation guardrails
* The A -> B shift is a **descriptive decomposition / composition exercise, not causal adjustment**: ASVAB and
  class rank are 1981 measures, partly *after* the 1979 grade-based outcome, and are themselves socially
  patterned (r = 0.48 with parental education). Adjusting for them conditions on a variable partly caused by
  the exposures.
* Outcome is post-secondary attendance/BA-track progress, right-censored by interview year; the dorm proxy misses
  commuter and community-college students, and the grade proxy is measured only once (1979).
* In the prospective variant (`y_PROSP`, N=10,856 -> 9,244 with ASVAB), where the academic measures strictly
  precede the outcome, the Black disadvantage is -5.7pp unadjusted (-6.0pp at equal sex/age), -3.0pp in Model A
  (AOR 0.574*), and only *partially* attenuated by ASVAB (-0.54 -> -0.21 log-odds, AOR 0.807, ns); the Hispanic
  coefficient still turns clearly positive (AOR 2.72). So with correct temporal ordering the Black gap shrinks but
  does not reverse, whereas the Hispanic advantage appears as soon as test scores are held constant.
* The composite `y_O3` inherits most of its racial pattern from the dorm component (`y_O2`), which is a
  residential-selection proxy: unadjusted Black gaps are -6.5pp on `y_O2` vs -5.5pp on `y_O1`.
* No survey weights; race cohort is nearly collinear with the NLSY sample strata -> unweighted, not design-based.

## 7. Two things that qualify the headline (added from diagnostics, not omitted)
**(a) The Model-A "no race gap" result is functional-form dependent** (`results/ses_functional_form_interaction.csv`).
The SES gradient is strongly non-linear (attended rate: 7.7% parent <=8, 7.7% 9-11, 16.5% 12, 31.8% 13-15,
45.3% 16, 61.0% >16 - flat below grade 12, steep above) and parental education is very differently distributed by
race (mean 9.1 years BLACK vs 11.9 NB NON-HISP). Black coefficient on y_O3 under different SES specifications:
linear pe AOR 0.96 (p=.62); categorical pe AOR 0.82 (p=.025); centred+quadratic 0.87 (p=.12). So Model A should be
read as "the Black gap is absorbed by family background to a degree that depends on how finely family background is
modelled", not as a clean zero. For `y_O2` and `y_PROSP` the Model-A Black deficit is robust to all three
specifications (AOR 0.51-0.65, all p<0.001).
**(b) There is a strong race x parental-education interaction**: Black x parent-ed AOR 0.80/yr (p<1e-6) on y_O3,
0.79 on y_O2, 0.74 on y_PROSP; Hispanic x parent-ed 0.94 (p=.016) on y_O3. The Black-vs-NBH deficit therefore
*widens* as parental education rises - which is exactly the pattern in the parental-education cross-tab
(-2.5pp at parent <=8, -20.9pp at parent 16+) and the reason the main effect "disappears" when the interaction and
the skewed education distribution are ignored.
**Model-family disagreements** (`results/model_family_agreement.csv`, 12 of 56 flagged cells): most are near-zero
race contrasts where logit and LPM pick opposite signs on differences below 1pp. Material exceptions: y_O3 Model A
Black (logit 0.99 ns vs LPM +0.033, p=.005) and y_O2/y_PROSP Model B Black (logit ~0.87-0.93 ns vs LPM +0.018/+0.022,
p<.01). Report those as statistically ambiguous rather than directional.

## 8. Final hypothesis (worded to the observable outcome)
> Accounting for academic characteristics changes the two background effects in opposite directions. In NLSY79,
> **post-secondary attendance / BA-track progress** (highest grade >= 13 by 1979, or dorm/fraternity/sorority
> residence in 1981-88; 19.8% of 12,532 respondents) is overwhelmingly predicted by family SES - each extra year of
> the better-educated parent's schooling raises the odds by a factor of 1.33 (95% CI 1.30-1.35; +33 percentage points
> comparing parents with <=8 versus >=16 years) - and adding parental education alone absorbs essentially all of the
> unadjusted racial gaps (Black -10.9pp, Hispanic -6.1pp, both reduced to about zero and non-significant). Adding
> 1981 ASVAB test scores then attenuates the SES effect by roughly a third of its log-odds (AOR 1.33 -> 1.18;
> -44% naive, -32% scale-corrected, -53% of the marginal effect; the parent-college advantage halves from +32pp to
> +17pp) while it leaves the effect large and highly significant, whereas the race coefficients do not attenuate at
> all - they reverse: Black AOR 0.96 -> 1.59 and Hispanic 1.05 -> 3.20 (+5pp and +15pp adjusted risk differences).
> So academic characteristics account for a substantial share of the socioeconomic gradient but *none* of the racial
> gap: minority respondents with equal parental schooling and equal test scores were at least as likely as their
> non-black non-Hispanic peers to progress to post-secondary education. What survives adjustment as the real racial
> divide is unequal academic preparation (mean ASVAB composite at parent education 16+: NB NON-HISP +0.91, BLACK
> +0.51, HISPANIC +0.10 SD units), plus a race x SES interaction (Black x parent-ed AOR 0.80/yr, p<1e-6) showing the
> Black deficit widening at high parental education. The Hispanic advantage is the most robust
> single finding (ASVAB-adjusted AOR 2.6-3.3 on the composite outcome, 2.7-3.1 in the correctly-ordered
> prospective variant, significant in every specification); the Black ASVAB-adjusted coefficient is much less
> stable - 1.0-1.6 on the composite outcome, 2.4-2.6 on the 1979-grade outcome, and 0.86-0.93 (ns) / 0.58 (LPM
> positive) in the college-residence and prospective variants - so the Black reversal should not be reported as a
> settled directional result.
> This is a descriptive composition/decomposition, not a causal estimate: ASVAB and class rank are 1981 measures that
> partly post-date the 1979 attainment measure and are themselves socially patterned (r = 0.48 with parental
> education), and the outcome is observed post-secondary attendance / BA-track progress, not a completed degree.
